WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.390
- Good afternoon everyone.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:10.530
Welcome to the first piece

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.840
of programming from the Yale Program,

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:17.840
the Study of Antisemitism
in the spring 2024 semester.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.010
I'm David Walsh.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.530
I'm the postdoctoral associate

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:25.173
at the program for the
study of antisemitism,

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:31.010
and I want to welcome this
afternoon Magda Teter,

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.080
who will be giving a talk, antisemitism

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.740
and racism, a shared history.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.720
Just introduce Professor Teter briefly.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.640
She is the Professor of History

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.750
and Shvidler Chair of Judaic
Studies at Fordham University.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:48.637
She's the author of "Jews and
Heretics in Catholic Poland",

00:00:48.637 --> 00:00:50.047
"Sinners on Trial",

00:00:50.047 --> 00:00:52.597
"Jews and Sacrilege
after the Reformation",

00:00:52.597 --> 00:00:56.010
"Blood Libel on the Trail
of an Antisemitic Myth",

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.530
which won the National
Jewish Book Award in 2020.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:00:59.910
It's fantastic book.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.470
I highly recommend it to you all.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:05.340
And most recently, "Christian
Supremacy Reckoning

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.190
with the Roots of
Antisemitism and Racism",

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.623
which was published in 2023.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.890
Professor Teter has received fellowships

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.980
from the John Simon Guggenheim
Memorial Foundation,

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.470
the Harry Frank Guggenheim Foundation,

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.090
the Harvard Radcliffe Institute,

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.771
the Coleman Center at the
New York Public Library,

00:01:23.771 --> 00:01:26.850
the National Endowment for the Humanities,

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.340
excuse me and others.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.560
Professor Teter is currently the president

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.110
of the American Academy
of Jewish Research.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:35.310
So we are very, very pleased

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:37.710
and very honored that she's with us today.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.760
And Professor Teter, please make it away.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.490
- Thank you so much for having me.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.980
I'm delighted to be with you to today

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:51.153
and to share my work
and my screen with you.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.433
So let me do that.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.840
So let me first say a few words about

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.280
how this book came about,

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.420
because I was trained as a historian

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.530
of Jewish history, a Jewish experience.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.800
My early work is in
Eastern Europe, Poland,

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.300
Polish Lithuanian Commonwealth.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:19.590
And then I expanded a
little bit geographically,

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:24.590
but this book really is one
that reaches across fields.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.660
And one of the reasons why
I was, I've been thinking

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.170
that we need to rethink

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:35.430
and find new approaches to
the study of antisemitism is

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.670
that I've been frustrated
with the historiography

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.320
of antisemitism.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.970
Scholars have been debating over

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.590
definitions over spelling,
what time periods,

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:52.710
what's the difference between
anti Judaism, anti-Jewish Jew,

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:55.683
anti Judaic antisemitism, Judeophobia.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:00.570
Is now, we're obviously
discussing the question

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.120
of anti-Zionism and antisemitism.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.950
Is this eternal hatred?

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.750
Is it a modern phenomenon?

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:10.980
And the these debates, you
know, producing lots of books

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:14.220
and articles and and
scholarly conversations

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:17.523
have never sort of failed
for me, that they are really,

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:20.490
really getting us somewhere.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:21.510
And I'm not the first one.

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:25.740
David Engel just told us to
get rid of all the definitions

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.130
and move and discuss what
was really happening.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:33.060
And then I had this sort of revelation

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.800
in 2016 when I was at the premier

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.690
of Raoul Peck's film,
"I Am Not Your Negro"

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:47.100
in which he play has this
clip of James Baldwin

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.382
and the PBS program, "The
Negro and the American Promise"

00:03:50.382 --> 00:03:55.382
in which Baldwin uses the N word

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:59.850
very emphatically and very
consciously, I will not use it.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:03.450
But saying, essentially
asking why is it, why was,

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:07.170
why it was necessary to have
the N in the first place?

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:08.940
Because I am not an N.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:10.884
I am a man.

00:04:10.884 --> 00:04:15.884
And if you think that I am N
it means that you need him.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:19.050
And if you, and I'm not the N here,

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.180
then you and you invented him,

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:22.980
you, the white people invented him,

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.870
then you have to find out why.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:28.807
And that when I saw the scene,
it was this big revelation

00:04:28.807 --> 00:04:32.760
and said, this is exactly like the Jew

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.060
invented by Christians.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:39.390
And just to give you a sense
of both what Baldwin meant

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:43.920
and what I mean by the sort
of caricature of a person,

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.830
of an image that doesn't really exist

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:49.260
and has nothing to do with the living

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:52.950
either in James Baldwin's
place, black people

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:56.430
or Jews in the case of the Jews.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:59.790
So the example of the, the N

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:04.560
and the creation of that
caricature in contrast

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.189
to whiteness is, are
some of the illustrations

00:05:09.189 --> 00:05:12.000
in a very, in a book

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.570
that actually argued for
against their humanity

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:17.073
of black people here.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:22.230
And here's another example,
juxtaposing the, you know,

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:26.850
white bodies against the
caricature black body.

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:28.833
And here is an example of a,

00:05:30.405 --> 00:05:34.230
this is a Nazi children's
book of the contrast

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:38.883
between the Aryan German and the Jew.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:44.720
Here is Fagan from, from "Oliver Twist".

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:49.440
And again, you can sort of see the girl

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.160
and the aesthetic of the girl
versus the representation

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:57.330
of the Jew and very similar terms

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:00.600
of presentation and contrasting.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:02.710
So I've been reading

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:07.410
and learning about black history

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:12.220
and American history
just for my own interest

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.870
because again I grew up in Europe

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:17.070
and then I was trained

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:19.620
in the United States in Jewish history.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:22.890
But on my own I was
reading on black history.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.350
And one of the things that kept,

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:28.020
I kept marking on the margins of the books

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.180
that I was reading is similarities between

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:35.400
the way black people were
discussed or described

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:38.970
or ideas that were associated with.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.640
And with that what was with Jews.

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:45.840
So we have, both of them
were obviously cursed by God

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.250
for different reasons, both
serve as these contract,

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:53.790
contrast figures constituted
either in the case

00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:56.640
of black people, of white identity

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:58.493
or in contrast of Jews, really important

00:06:58.493 --> 00:07:01.020
and constitutive to Christianity

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:04.200
and Christian deity.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.870
Both were seen as lazy

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:11.073
for different reason, but
both at the expense of,

00:07:11.997 --> 00:07:13.920
of, in Jews case the Christians

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:18.123
and in the case of black
people the white population.

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:22.410
They were both seen as in different ways

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:27.240
as arrogant, insolent or uppity

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:31.080
in moments when they were
trying to assert the rights

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:33.093
or their dignity.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:39.150
They were both in modern
times seen as ineligible

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:40.827
for citizenship because of,

00:07:40.827 --> 00:07:43.375
and this is for the people generous

00:07:43.375 --> 00:07:48.180
and willing eventually to maybe grant Jews

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:51.513
and black people citizenship for,

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:54.660
after centuries of oppression

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:59.100
and this sort of uplift persuasion idea.

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:02.553
Both were discussed as a
question, as a problem.

00:08:03.665 --> 00:08:07.780
And in the modern times they were both

00:08:08.731 --> 00:08:11.670
refused to be accepted as equal citizen.

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:14.790
And when the law changed

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:19.563
and both eventually reluctantly
were accepted as citizens,

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:23.910
there was an immediate backlash.

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:26.913
So, and they were both
also in terms of the,

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.700
seen as carnal and dangerous.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:31.380
And here I'm going to show you a couple

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:33.390
of other images here.

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:36.870
Here are some images of the
lecherous dangerous black man,

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:38.493
dangerous to white women.

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:44.360
And there is an example of those images

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:47.310
of the lecherous Jew.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:51.690
And again, in case of Black Americans,

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:55.293
we know that this often
ended in lynchings.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:00.540
The one case we know of
similar dynamic in America

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:03.240
of Leo Frank was also related to that.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:08.240
But in Europe, that dynamic
was seen in different ways

00:09:09.431 --> 00:09:13.593
and expressed in violence
also in different ways.

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:19.790
So in that, in my, in both
sort of seeing these parallels

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.590
and in my frustration with the field

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:24.720
of antisemitism studies,

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:27.480
I've been sort of searching
for understanding.

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:31.560
And I thought that the
parallels are so uncanny

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:33.990
that they needed some explanation.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:38.990
And the longest hatred
idea that that has been

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:43.530
so dominant in the
scholarship on antisemitism

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:45.510
or the modernity of antisemitism

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:48.813
did not quite feel satisfying.

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:51.736
So as I've been teaching

00:09:51.736 --> 00:09:55.840
and again, the value of
comparison was actually

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:02.460
again, not something that
I invented W.E.B Du Bois

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:04.920
after visiting Europe several times,

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:08.163
and then the Warsaw Ghetto 1948,

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:14.000
he wrote his essay on "The
Negro and the Warsaw Ghetto"

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:20.360
in which he highlighted the
value of his experiences

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:21.780
that it helped him not

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:24.630
so much give him not so
much clearer understanding

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:27.630
of the Jewish problem in
the world as it was a real

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:29.970
and more complete understanding
of the Negro problem.

00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:34.080
So for me, engaging with
black studies has given me,

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:38.940
I think a clearer understanding
of the Jewish experience

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:43.940
and Jew and antisemitism
or anti-Jewish animus

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:48.210
by engaging and thinking along the way

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:51.333
and reading about anti-black racism.

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:55.710
In the '30s and 40's other scholars,

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:59.220
both Jewish and black intellectuals

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:04.220
were making connections
between the two in essays

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:10.080
and in case of some of black intellectuals

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:14.275
to draw attention to what was happening

00:11:14.275 --> 00:11:18.450
to black people in America
through the experience

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:23.450
and concern that was raised
about the Nazi treatment of Jews

00:11:23.597 --> 00:11:26.460
before the Holocaust
in the 1930s especially

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:27.903
and then afterwards.

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:32.857
In 1948, a really wonderful film,

00:11:32.857 --> 00:11:35.400
"Strange Victory" by Leo Hurwitz,

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:39.120
which I call a poetic meditation

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:40.740
on racism and antisemitism.

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:43.140
And I know that seems strange,

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:48.140
but it's a really poetic film
that brings these two together

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:53.510
and sort of combines the
both the experience in Europe

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:58.860
and the current, what was
current in 1948 experiences

00:11:58.860 --> 00:11:59.910
of black Americans,

00:11:59.910 --> 00:12:04.383
and also the persistence of
antisemitism in America as well.

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:08.460
So one of the ways that thinking

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:13.460
and reading about racism is that

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:17.880
it often focuses on structures on law,

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:22.680
on oppression coming not just from ideas

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:27.680
and culture, although
culture is in broadly,

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:32.376
but also from within the role, the law

00:12:32.376 --> 00:12:35.700
and again, structural racism plays.

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:39.150
And that is always seen in the context

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:40.943
of power and oppression.

00:12:40.943 --> 00:12:45.943
And here that help me think through

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:48.060
some of the languages

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:51.450
and hierarchies that emerge in the

00:12:51.450 --> 00:12:54.390
Christian theology first about Jews,

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:58.680
but then later on have an impact on law

00:12:58.680 --> 00:12:59.940
and legal framework

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:03.360
and legal position of
Jews in both pre-modern

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:05.100
and modern period.

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:08.580
So let me flesh out the theology,

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:10.080
and I'm not going to be looking actually

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:12.420
with the traditional texts that focus

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:14.730
on the crucifixion or Judas

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:19.730
or other parts of the
Christian scriptures,

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:24.060
but actually on Paul and his letters,

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:27.030
because to me they play a crucial role

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:32.030
in the later on framing
of social hierarchies

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:36.420
and habits of thinking about
Jews and also legal frameworks.

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:40.870
So Paul establishes a hierarchy of values

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:46.940
by juxtaposing law that for him is Judaism

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:52.890
and faith that for him is
this emergent Christianity

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:57.890
and uses the framework of the
two sons that Abraham had,

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:05.790
Ishmael and Isaac, one as
he says by a slave woman,

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:09.330
the other by a free woman to cast them

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:12.930
as types for Judaism and Christianity.

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:16.260
So one, the child of a slave
born according to flesh

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:20.640
and the other child of the free
woman born through promise.

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:24.870
So Ishmael through flesh
from a slave woman from Hagar

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:28.263
and Isaac from a child of promise.

00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:33.690
And he says there are two
symbols of the two covenants,

00:14:33.690 --> 00:14:37.800
Mount Sinai Judaism bearing
children for slavery.

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:42.390
And Hagar now is, that is the Hagar

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:44.440
and then the present Jerusalem

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:48.660
is the present Jerusalem in slavery

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:53.056
and Jerusalem above is the free woman.

00:14:53.056 --> 00:14:57.360
And that is Christianity.

00:14:57.360 --> 00:15:01.410
So freedom is associated
with Christianity,

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:06.410
slavery and servitude with
Judaism and Jerusalem.

00:15:08.674 --> 00:15:10.800
And a again, he returns to it.

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:13.050
This means that not the
children of the flesh

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:14.490
who are the children of God,

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:17.220
but the children of
promise that is not Jews

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:21.870
who are observing the commandments of God

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:25.480
as they were given, but the newly forming

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:29.310
Christianity through faith and promise.

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:34.310
And then he uses in a
totally non-social meaning,

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:37.620
but in a theological meaning, a phrase,

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:39.180
the elder shall serve the anger,

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:41.700
which comes from the book of Genesis,

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:45.390
which will really contribute

00:15:45.390 --> 00:15:50.390
to the creation of legal
hierarchies in Christian Europe.

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:56.250
There were, I'm jumping
centuries in the book,

00:15:56.250 --> 00:15:58.290
I do a little bit more detail,

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:02.803
but Augustine returns to
the motive of the contrast

00:16:04.650 --> 00:16:08.070
between carnality and spirit flesh

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:13.070
and spirit and promise
and flesh and so on.

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:16.800
And elaborates more on the different pairs

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:21.030
of the brothers in the Bible.

00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:25.230
And goes on to detail to explain the verse

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:27.840
what now at the time where he lives,

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:31.800
which is when the Roman
empire is now a formerly

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:35.640
Christian empire, what in
his new historical context,

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:38.160
the elder shall serve the younger means.

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:42.060
So he says, in the city of
God as to the statement,

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:45.240
the elder shall serve the younger scarcely

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:47.220
among anyone must think,

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:50.220
understands it in any other
way than the elder people

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:55.220
of the Jews should serve the
younger Christian people.

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:58.530
And then again, he returned
what could be clear

