And as the modern nations at
the end of the 18th century,

00:33:49.740 --> 00:33:52.653
we're inventing the new idea of the we,

00:33:53.850 --> 00:33:57.960
we the people and seeking what this meant.

00:33:57.960 --> 00:34:00.990
The question was whether
Jews and black people

00:34:00.990 --> 00:34:02.580
belong in the new we.

00:34:02.580 --> 00:34:05.460
And in fact, during the French Revolution,

00:34:05.460 --> 00:34:07.860
the same people that were
debating the citizenship

00:34:07.860 --> 00:34:12.720
about Jews were also debating
the eligibility of black

00:34:12.720 --> 00:34:15.240
and free black people,
not just enslaved people,

00:34:15.240 --> 00:34:17.763
but free black people for citizenship.

00:34:18.690 --> 00:34:23.610
So this is certainly an issue.

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:28.610
And Jews by that time
are already racialized

00:34:28.920 --> 00:34:32.550
as Oriental and as coming from Asia.

00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:36.600
And the question becomes
whether they can not just as,

00:34:36.600 --> 00:34:39.210
not just on the religious basis.

00:34:39.210 --> 00:34:44.210
Also, as you can see in the
Willhelm, Michaelis text,

00:34:44.790 --> 00:34:47.580
the law of Moses, does the law of Moses

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:49.740
makes citizenship in full integration

00:34:49.740 --> 00:34:50.730
of the Jews impossible.

00:34:50.730 --> 00:34:52.290
I think it does.

00:34:52.290 --> 00:34:55.290
They will never become fully
integrated the way Catholics,

00:34:55.290 --> 00:34:57.900
Lutherans, Germans, Wends and
French live together, right?

00:34:57.900 --> 00:35:02.900
The religion ostensibly prevents
Jews from this integration.

00:35:04.230 --> 00:35:07.590
But there's also this
racial, they stem from Asia

00:35:07.590 --> 00:35:09.483
and differ from others.

00:35:10.890 --> 00:35:15.390
But the issue was not human rights

00:35:15.390 --> 00:35:17.850
and civil rights at
some level of residents,

00:35:17.850 --> 00:35:21.210
but really political rights,
the equality of rights

00:35:21.210 --> 00:35:24.630
as citizens and active citizens.

00:35:24.630 --> 00:35:27.390
The same questions were
in the Netherlands.

00:35:27.390 --> 00:35:30.870
Do we continue regard Jews
as people, as alien residents

00:35:30.870 --> 00:35:32.733
or regard them as Dutchmen?

00:35:33.630 --> 00:35:37.650
Are they fellow beings or are they equal

00:35:37.650 --> 00:35:39.933
or on equal footing with Dutchman?

00:35:41.280 --> 00:35:42.990
And again, collectively speaking,

00:35:42.990 --> 00:35:47.880
Jews cannot share in our Dutch
social rights as citizens,

00:35:47.880 --> 00:35:49.410
as long as they are Jews, right?

00:35:49.410 --> 00:35:51.300
There's this religion, Jewishness

00:35:51.300 --> 00:35:53.103
and religion preventing them.

00:35:53.940 --> 00:35:58.560
And the one of the members
of the debate said,

00:35:58.560 --> 00:36:01.920
well, that we need to decide

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:04.260
whether we in the
Netherlands honor the rights

00:36:04.260 --> 00:36:08.820
of men or only a Christian
men, the rights of citizens

00:36:08.820 --> 00:36:11.670
or only of rights of Christian citizens.

00:36:11.670 --> 00:36:15.330
So again, this is around religion

00:36:15.330 --> 00:36:18.270
protecting the Christian
supremacy in Europe.

00:36:18.270 --> 00:36:21.540
But there is this element of
racialization already then,

00:36:21.540 --> 00:36:26.540
and it will become more
apparent in the 19th century.

00:36:27.120 --> 00:36:29.580
But again, these debates over Jews

00:36:29.580 --> 00:36:32.610
and people who are debating it,

00:36:32.610 --> 00:36:37.200
keep the question of people
of color, black people

00:36:37.200 --> 00:36:39.510
as well in their minds.

00:36:39.510 --> 00:36:43.370
And here is from the same
debate about Jewish citizenship

00:36:43.370 --> 00:36:48.090
in the Netherlands on a
reference to the slaves

00:36:48.090 --> 00:36:52.380
of San Domingo, that
it was too fast, right?

00:36:52.380 --> 00:36:55.770
Jews shouldn't be given the
citizenship rights too fast.

00:36:55.770 --> 00:36:59.430
They should be, the pace should be slower

00:36:59.430 --> 00:37:03.030
because look at what
happened in San Domingo.

00:37:03.030 --> 00:37:06.750
The untimely equality
granted to the slaves

00:37:06.750 --> 00:37:08.610
of San Domingo served to the destruction

00:37:08.610 --> 00:37:10.233
of that French population.

00:37:12.060 --> 00:37:15.480
And again, it continues that
is a Jew, Christian rights

00:37:15.480 --> 00:37:18.840
for whom were these rights developed.

00:37:18.840 --> 00:37:22.743
Bruno Bauer, who sees Jews as a problem,

00:37:23.820 --> 00:37:27.000
says that this was all,
that debate was really only

00:37:27.000 --> 00:37:32.000
for the Christian world, not for Jews.

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:37.050
Wilhelm Marr, the person
behind the Antisemitic League

00:37:37.050 --> 00:37:38.610
and therefore the new term,

00:37:38.610 --> 00:37:41.010
the neologism antisemitism.

00:37:41.010 --> 00:37:43.650
Again affirms that Jews are Semitic race

00:37:43.650 --> 00:37:46.860
completely different with
homeland and Palestine,

00:37:46.860 --> 00:37:51.317
and then turns into the
question of political power.

00:37:52.590 --> 00:37:55.170
And in fact, he affirms that when it comes

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:57.210
to religious persecutions,

00:37:57.210 --> 00:38:01.020
I even take the Jews under
my unconditional protection.

00:38:01.020 --> 00:38:03.990
But it was about sharing
the political power that,

00:38:03.990 --> 00:38:08.130
but that political equality
that in his mind led

00:38:08.130 --> 00:38:11.340
to alien Jewish domination.

00:38:11.340 --> 00:38:14.250
And he turns into voting

00:38:14.250 --> 00:38:18.930
and electoral power electing
the alien he calls masters

00:38:18.930 --> 00:38:22.170
and making them legislators
and judges, right?

00:38:22.170 --> 00:38:24.150
Jews in power.

00:38:24.150 --> 00:38:29.150
Similarly, a German historian
Heinrih von Treitschke

00:38:33.600 --> 00:38:36.210
refers to emancipation or the granting

00:38:36.210 --> 00:38:38.940
of citizenship to Jews.

00:38:38.940 --> 00:38:43.940
And his again, discomfort with
the literal parity, right?

00:38:44.130 --> 00:38:48.150
He's, they demanded the literal parity,

00:38:48.150 --> 00:38:50.850
but we Germans are after
all Christian nation

00:38:50.850 --> 00:38:53.580
and Jews are only a minority.

00:38:53.580 --> 00:38:57.150
So that habit of thinking of
Jews as inferior to Christians

00:38:57.150 --> 00:39:01.710
that we've seen developing over
centuries is playing itself

00:39:01.710 --> 00:39:06.090
out in a new political
situation of Jews as citizens.

00:39:06.090 --> 00:39:09.000
And again, Heinrich von Treitschke

00:39:09.000 --> 00:39:11.855
also racializes Jews as Orientals.

00:39:11.855 --> 00:39:14.760
And that racialization
goes back actually to these

00:39:14.760 --> 00:39:19.760
religious debates of the
17th early modern period

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:24.870
where Jews are, Judaism
is typically discussed

00:39:24.870 --> 00:39:29.640
in the context of biblical
observances of Jews.

00:39:29.640 --> 00:39:33.817
And therefore they are
placed in Palestine,

00:39:36.420 --> 00:39:39.840
right, in the Holy Land, not in Europe.

00:39:39.840 --> 00:39:44.840
And these books tend to be
kind of proto evolutionary.

00:39:45.270 --> 00:39:46.675
They show the evolution

00:39:46.675 --> 00:39:51.675
of different western different religions.

00:39:53.730 --> 00:39:55.320
And Judaism is always the sort

00:39:55.320 --> 00:39:57.900
of proto religion from
which emerge Christianity

00:39:57.900 --> 00:40:00.660
in different denominations
of Christianity.

00:40:00.660 --> 00:40:05.520
And then there are all
these other non-Western

00:40:05.520 --> 00:40:08.970
groups which also have animal sacrifices

00:40:08.970 --> 00:40:10.290
and do different things.

00:40:10.290 --> 00:40:15.270
In fact, one book in 1705
juxtaposed the customs

00:40:15.270 --> 00:40:16.320
of East Indians.

00:40:16.320 --> 00:40:20.550
So in India, in Asia with those of Jews

00:40:20.550 --> 00:40:22.500
and Jews of antiquity.

00:40:22.500 --> 00:40:27.300
And here is a page showing the charming

00:40:27.300 --> 00:40:32.300
of the serpents is
supposedly happening in India

00:40:32.640 --> 00:40:36.630
and comparing it to Moses and his staff

00:40:36.630 --> 00:40:41.430
and the serpent in the Bible.

00:40:41.430 --> 00:40:46.430
And that of course leads to
exclusion Jews to Palestina,

00:40:46.680 --> 00:40:48.030
Jews to Palestine.

00:40:48.030 --> 00:40:51.275
You can see again that that racialization

00:40:51.275 --> 00:40:55.163
and the caricature of
Jews as these ugly people

00:40:57.300 --> 00:41:00.330
not the Jew, the caricature Jew

00:41:00.330 --> 00:41:04.263
that get me thinking
after a Baldwin squad.

00:41:05.640 --> 00:41:09.390
So again, that kind of
process of de-Europeanization

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:12.870
of Jews, of seeing them as alien,

00:41:12.870 --> 00:41:15.180
as not being part of Europe

00:41:15.180 --> 00:41:18.720
leads to that social exclusion

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:22.950
and political exclusion as well.

00:41:22.950 --> 00:41:26.940
And if the debate was
framed around religion

00:41:26.940 --> 00:41:31.320
or racialized religion in
Europe, in the United States,

00:41:31.320 --> 00:41:35.640
that debate is created
is along racial lines.

00:41:35.640 --> 00:41:40.610
And here we have the first
kind of genuine debate about

00:41:40.610 --> 00:41:43.500
what citizenship meant
in the United States.

00:41:43.500 --> 00:41:45.810
The word exists, but it's not defined.

00:41:45.810 --> 00:41:47.190
In the Missouri debate.

00:41:47.190 --> 00:41:50.730
and one of the
representatives from Virginia

00:41:50.730 --> 00:41:52.140
spoke in the language.

00:41:52.140 --> 00:41:54.750
This is what, when I
read it sounded like we,

00:41:54.750 --> 00:41:56.640
the white people, we the Christian people,

00:41:56.640 --> 00:41:59.040
how it is very similar to the echo to me.

00:41:59.040 --> 00:42:02.580
Indian, free Negroes,
mulatto slaves tell me not

00:42:02.580 --> 00:42:04.470
that the constitutional when it speaks

00:42:04.470 --> 00:42:07.440
of we the people means these.

00:42:07.440 --> 00:42:08.820
The argument in favor

00:42:08.820 --> 00:42:11.100
and including the class of of citizens,

00:42:11.100 --> 00:42:15.240
free people of color goes too far.

00:42:15.240 --> 00:42:18.180
They of course meant we
the white people, right?

00:42:18.180 --> 00:42:22.470
They were European descendants,
so that could not have been.

00:42:22.470 --> 00:42:25.800
And again, can negro whose ancestors

00:42:25.800 --> 00:42:27.330
were important to this country

00:42:27.330 --> 00:42:31.920
and so the slaves become a
member of a political community.

00:42:31.920 --> 00:42:34.800
Think about can the Jews

00:42:34.800 --> 00:42:39.360
and their law can become
part of the citizens

00:42:39.360 --> 00:42:42.390
and equal rights and
be unequal parts as us.

00:42:42.390 --> 00:42:45.900
Can they be entitled, become
entitled to all the rights

00:42:45.900 --> 00:42:49.290
of privileges immunities
guaranteed that by

00:42:49.290 --> 00:42:51.423
that instrument of the citizen.

00:42:52.440 --> 00:42:56.280
So what we see is that as
we've seen the modern idea

00:42:56.280 --> 00:43:00.030
of equality, ideal of equality clashes

00:43:00.030 --> 00:43:03.049
with the established social hierarchies

00:43:03.049 --> 00:43:08.049
and essentially they come to
the conclusion that no Jews

00:43:08.160 --> 00:43:11.100
and black people do not belong to the we.

00:43:11.100 --> 00:43:15.120
And again, and when they do attain

00:43:15.120 --> 00:43:19.500
legal equality forced in
America through civil war

00:43:19.500 --> 00:43:23.040
and in the united, in
Europe reluctantly granted

00:43:23.040 --> 00:43:28.040
through the legal transformations
of European states,

00:43:28.110 --> 00:43:31.638
that equality is seen
as unearned, usurped,

00:43:31.638 --> 00:43:34.350
and out of place.

00:43:34.350 --> 00:43:38.670
And we see this today
in contemporary echoes

00:43:38.670 --> 00:43:42.903
of that exclusion in
inferiority and power.

00:43:44.010 --> 00:43:49.010
This is a shot from a ad you
can find on YouTube from 2018

00:43:52.650 --> 00:43:55.770
about highlighting the
connoisseur of chaos,

00:43:55.770 --> 00:43:57.750
George Soros funding the left

00:43:57.750 --> 00:44:01.050
and causing all the sort of racial unrest

00:44:01.050 --> 00:44:05.670
that draws on an earlier tropo
Jews causing racial unrest

00:44:05.670 --> 00:44:07.593
in the civil rights era.

00:44:08.910 --> 00:44:11.580
Race mixing is financed and led by Jews.

00:44:11.580 --> 00:44:15.060
Again, very similar idea behind it.

00:44:15.060 --> 00:44:20.060
And more recently we've
seen it in the case

00:44:20.141 --> 00:44:25.141
of district attorney Alvin Bragg

00:44:25.200 --> 00:44:27.900
who is black in Manhattan,

00:44:27.900 --> 00:44:32.900
when he charged Donald Trump with crimes.

00:44:35.220 --> 00:44:37.650
That's one of those demonstrated,

00:44:37.650 --> 00:44:41.853
George Soros funds D.A. Bragg, Google it.

00:44:43.350 --> 00:44:46.860
So I wanna share a few concluding thoughts

00:44:46.860 --> 00:44:51.860
on how thinking and
expanding and moving out

00:44:52.110 --> 00:44:55.050
and engaging with other fields

00:44:55.050 --> 00:44:58.320
is really helpful for one's own field.

00:44:58.320 --> 00:45:01.770
It's very difficult and it's very,

00:45:01.770 --> 00:45:05.310
feels very one feels very vulnerable

00:45:05.310 --> 00:45:08.970
exploring and a field in
which one is not trained.

00:45:08.970 --> 00:45:11.910
You sort of realize how many
things we observe over decades

00:45:11.910 --> 00:45:16.380
of work and how you, you
can navigate your own field,

00:45:16.380 --> 00:45:20.700
but when you engage with
a new field, you learn,

00:45:20.700 --> 00:45:24.180
you have to learn not
just, you know, facts,

00:45:24.180 --> 00:45:27.513
but also historiographic
debate and so on so forth.

00:45:28.380 --> 00:45:30.480
But why I think thinking

00:45:30.480 --> 00:45:32.880
black studies really helped me rethink

00:45:32.880 --> 00:45:34.320
antisemitism two ways.

00:45:34.320 --> 00:45:38.790
A, moving beyond the
emotional history of emotion

00:45:38.790 --> 00:45:41.130
of history, of hate, you know,

00:45:41.130 --> 00:45:43.830
the longest hatred in that way,

00:45:43.830 --> 00:45:46.740
but thinking along the structures and laws

00:45:46.740 --> 00:45:49.503
and oppression and power.

00:45:50.460 --> 00:45:53.580
But also, and that became really clear

00:45:53.580 --> 00:45:56.550
after October 7th,

00:45:56.550 --> 00:46:01.550
is that black studies and
highlight the experience

00:46:03.150 --> 00:46:05.940
of black people under influence

00:46:05.940 --> 00:46:08.820
and impacted by racism

00:46:08.820 --> 00:46:13.820
is the scholars focus on
what racism does to people,

00:46:16.290 --> 00:46:19.440
how it oppress, how that
oppression functions

00:46:19.440 --> 00:46:23.610
and how it impacts those whom it targets.

00:46:23.610 --> 00:46:28.290
The field of antisemitism
focuses on antisemites,

00:46:28.290 --> 00:46:32.190
analyzes antisemites think and repeats

00:46:32.190 --> 00:46:36.270
and traces the antisemitic idea.

00:46:36.270 --> 00:46:39.840
We do not, we have not paid
attention to the structures

00:46:39.840 --> 00:46:41.490
of law and power,

00:46:41.490 --> 00:46:43.860
and we have not, as
scholars paid attention

00:46:43.860 --> 00:46:48.860
to what antisemitism or
anti-Jewish animus more broadly

00:46:49.080 --> 00:46:53.850
beyond the modern period does to Jews

00:46:53.850 --> 00:46:57.870
and to their experience,
to their bodily experience.

00:46:57.870 --> 00:47:00.330
We studied the Jew

00:47:00.330 --> 00:47:02.700
and how the Jew, the image of the Jew,

00:47:02.700 --> 00:47:04.350
the caricature emerges.

00:47:04.350 --> 00:47:05.640
So I'm going to stop here

00:47:05.640 --> 00:47:08.550
so there is a little time for discussion

00:47:08.550 --> 00:47:11.373
and I look forward to
hearing what you have to say.

00:47:14.700 --> 00:47:17.220
- Thank you, Professor
Teter, that was a really,

00:47:17.220 --> 00:47:19.800
really fantastic and thought provoking

00:47:19.800 --> 00:47:23.280
and there's several great
questions already in the queue.

00:47:23.280 --> 00:47:24.990
So for those of you who have never come

00:47:24.990 --> 00:47:27.090
to a Yetzer event online before,

00:47:27.090 --> 00:47:30.750
please put your questions
in the Q&amp;A chat feature.

00:47:30.750 --> 00:47:32.910
I forgot to say this in my introduction,

00:47:32.910 --> 00:47:33.935
you could have been doing this

00:47:33.935 --> 00:47:36.780
and some of you were doing
it all throughout the talk,

00:47:36.780 --> 00:47:41.780
and we have about 10 to 15
minutes, so we'll just dive in.

00:47:41.940 --> 00:47:45.030
The first question, thank
you for this discussion.

00:47:45.030 --> 00:47:47.130
Can you please shed light
on how Jews of color,

00:47:47.130 --> 00:47:48.630
especially black Jews,

00:47:48.630 --> 00:47:50.550
factor into your comparative
analysis while being

00:47:50.550 --> 00:47:53.400
at the intersection of both
marginalized identities?

00:47:53.400 --> 00:47:56.937
- This is a great question
because I think the,

00:47:56.937 --> 00:48:00.760
and this is especially true for the later

00:48:01.860 --> 00:48:05.700
or more recent, you
know, now a few decades,

00:48:05.700 --> 00:48:10.700
but relatively recent in
American Jewish historiography,

00:48:12.810 --> 00:48:15.270
the question of sort of Jewish whiteness

00:48:15.270 --> 00:48:17.310
and you know, how the Jews become white,

00:48:17.310 --> 00:48:19.503
the price of whiteness and all that.

00:48:20.760 --> 00:48:25.620
And and I do talk about it
from a legal perspective.

00:48:25.620 --> 00:48:30.620
I use Ian Haney Lopez's
phrase, white by law,

00:48:31.680 --> 00:48:34.110
that is that in the United States,

00:48:34.110 --> 00:48:36.480
because Jews could immigrate

00:48:36.480 --> 00:48:39.123
and be naturalized,
they were white by law,

00:48:39.123 --> 00:48:40.860
they were considered like white by law.

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:45.360
There were other obviously forms

00:48:45.360 --> 00:48:47.880
of discrimination against Jews in Maryland

00:48:47.880 --> 00:48:50.100
and some other places.

00:48:50.100 --> 00:48:51.570
There may have been even restrictions

00:48:51.570 --> 00:48:53.760
on voting in other places.

00:48:53.760 --> 00:48:56.970
But from a national perspective,

00:48:56.970 --> 00:49:01.770
Jews were, could immigrate and
were considered white by law.

00:49:01.770 --> 00:49:05.340
That's, and Laura Lipman, Arnold Lipman

00:49:05.340 --> 00:49:09.150
has shown a very interesting
tale of some Jews

00:49:09.150 --> 00:49:12.690
from the colonies going to England sort

00:49:12.690 --> 00:49:17.640
of becoming certified as white

00:49:17.640 --> 00:49:19.350
or European at least coming

00:49:19.350 --> 00:49:24.350
into the United States
and being naturalized.

00:49:25.200 --> 00:49:29.253
But in a, the point that
I wanted to make is that,

00:49:30.480 --> 00:49:35.250
that that discourse of Jewish whiteness,

00:49:35.250 --> 00:49:37.353
which I think is very problematic,

00:49:38.550 --> 00:49:43.510
is also erasing the
experience of Jews of color

00:49:44.580 --> 00:49:47.700
who as the the questionnaire mentioned

00:49:47.700 --> 00:49:49.440
are sort of bridging it.

00:49:49.440 --> 00:49:52.470
And that experience,
again, I'm not an expert

00:49:52.470 --> 00:49:56.790
and I, there are scholars
of written about it,

00:49:56.790 --> 00:50:01.080
but they, Jews of color,
especially black Jews

00:50:01.080 --> 00:50:03.990
are caught in that, especially in America.

00:50:03.990 --> 00:50:05.280
And again, in that America.

00:50:05.280 --> 00:50:07.590
It is very different in Israel,

00:50:07.590 --> 00:50:12.590
there's a very different
social hierarchy there.

00:50:12.600 --> 00:50:16.147
But in America they were not

00:50:18.646 --> 00:50:23.646
quite seen as Jewish by European Jews.

00:50:23.821 --> 00:50:27.030
And they were not quite seen as,

00:50:27.030 --> 00:50:30.090
as having the experience
of black Americans.

00:50:30.090 --> 00:50:35.013
And so there is this sort
of in-betweenness here.

00:50:36.120 --> 00:50:37.260
- Yeah.

00:50:37.260 --> 00:50:40.470
There are two additional
questions that are sort

00:50:40.470 --> 00:50:42.330
of along the same theme,
although we might have already

00:50:42.330 --> 00:50:44.730
addressed the thrust of it.

00:50:44.730 --> 00:50:49.080
One on how black and Jewish
communities can build solidarity

00:50:49.080 --> 00:50:52.710
amongst each other and another on this,

00:50:52.710 --> 00:50:54.960
the consequences of this shift

00:50:54.960 --> 00:50:58.110
in the Jewish whiteness question

00:50:58.110 --> 00:50:59.880
and the specific phrasing is in the eyes

00:50:59.880 --> 00:51:02.370
of many black citizens and
theorists, Jews are white.

00:51:02.370 --> 00:51:05.284
When did this shift occur
and its consequences?

00:51:05.284 --> 00:51:07.773
- Yeah and certainly,

00:51:09.630 --> 00:51:13.413
I mean James Baldwin
has this famous essay,

00:51:14.580 --> 00:51:19.580
and I don't remember the exact
title, but blacks hate Jews

00:51:19.871 --> 00:51:23.512
not because they're,
blacks are antisemitic,

00:51:23.512 --> 00:51:24.715
because they're Jews,

00:51:24.715 --> 00:51:25.650
because they white or something.

00:51:25.650 --> 00:51:27.576
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:51:27.576 --> 00:51:28.980
Something to that effect.
- Something like that.

00:51:28.980 --> 00:51:31.920
From the 1960s and one,

00:51:31.920 --> 00:51:35.490
and you know, he's essentially
questioning the position

00:51:35.490 --> 00:51:38.280
of American Jews and immigrants

00:51:38.280 --> 00:51:42.008
and the United States as
white and what it does.

00:51:42.008 --> 00:51:44.310
And there is this breakdown, right?

00:51:44.310 --> 00:51:48.510
Even the 1930s and 1940s,
these intellectuals

00:51:48.510 --> 00:51:50.280
are engaging with each other

00:51:50.280 --> 00:51:53.820
by 1960s we have a breakdown of that.

00:51:53.820 --> 00:51:58.200
And one of the questions that I pose

00:51:58.200 --> 00:52:02.880
is this is certainly the case,

00:52:02.880 --> 00:52:07.560
but why is James Baldwin
singling out Jews?

00:52:07.560 --> 00:52:09.600
Right, he's obviously a,

00:52:09.600 --> 00:52:12.183
writes in many places about whiteness,

00:52:14.220 --> 00:52:18.237
but I'm not, I know he
mentioned Irish here

00:52:18.237 --> 00:52:20.550
and there and he mentioned
Italian that, you know,

00:52:20.550 --> 00:52:23.400
they arrive and they become
white in the United States,

00:52:23.400 --> 00:52:27.750
and all of us actually
become white when we,

00:52:27.750 --> 00:52:31.380
when we're filling all of us,
all of us of this complexion

00:52:31.380 --> 00:52:33.000
that I have become white

00:52:33.000 --> 00:52:34.890
when we fill out census and other things.

00:52:34.890 --> 00:52:38.103
The United States law
functions along those sort of,

00:52:39.390 --> 00:52:42.450
even if we don't identify
in racial terms, right?

00:52:42.450 --> 00:52:47.450
So yes, everybody becomes
white, yet he focuses on Jews.

00:52:47.700 --> 00:52:52.590
And to me that also signals

00:52:52.590 --> 00:52:56.400
a kind of discomfort with Jewish whiteness

00:52:56.400 --> 00:53:00.480
and perhaps an unacknowledged,

00:53:00.480 --> 00:53:03.420
and I'm not just talking
here about Baldwin,

00:53:03.420 --> 00:53:06.693
but unacknowledged objection to Jews,

00:53:13.380 --> 00:53:15.330
exercising the rights of citizens.

00:53:15.330 --> 00:53:18.030
And yes, citizens should in America

00:53:18.030 --> 00:53:22.980
was framed along racial
lines and whiteness,

00:53:22.980 --> 00:53:25.780
but when we reframed
the question that Jews

00:53:26.940 --> 00:53:30.660
are coming, as all immigrants are coming

00:53:30.660 --> 00:53:32.490
and trying to exercise their right

00:53:32.490 --> 00:53:35.670
as citizen rather than achieve whiteness,

00:53:35.670 --> 00:53:37.260
that might change discourse.

00:53:37.260 --> 00:53:42.220
But then that may also
change the perception

00:53:43.680 --> 00:53:45.960
or it may highlight the discomfort

00:53:45.960 --> 00:53:47.670
with Jewish equality as well

00:53:47.670 --> 00:53:52.320
that I think there's is a
play a little bit in there.

00:53:52.320 --> 00:53:55.500
That the fact that Jews are singled out

00:53:55.500 --> 00:53:57.160
in that discourse of whiteness

00:53:58.260 --> 00:54:01.530
over other groups that are also, you know,

00:54:01.530 --> 00:54:05.010
coming and are white signals to me,

00:54:05.010 --> 00:54:06.840
there is something going on in that way.

00:54:06.840 --> 00:54:08.460
And of course there is these expectations

00:54:08.460 --> 00:54:11.520
that an oppressed would have a solidarity

00:54:11.520 --> 00:54:13.740
with other oppress and so on and so forth

00:54:13.740 --> 00:54:18.224
that plays plays a role.

00:54:18.224 --> 00:54:20.520
And there is a breakdown.

00:54:20.520 --> 00:54:23.220
There are all kinds of
complexity issues after,

00:54:23.220 --> 00:54:25.803
especially after the civil rights era.

00:54:27.090 --> 00:54:31.330
Sometimes, you know,
talking past each other's

00:54:32.460 --> 00:54:35.400
historical experiences,

00:54:35.400 --> 00:54:38.070
the objections to affirmative actions

00:54:38.070 --> 00:54:41.280
at some Jewish groups had,

00:54:41.280 --> 00:54:43.830
had a very different
historical meaning for Jews,

00:54:43.830 --> 00:54:46.290
especially many of whom
were immigrants from Europe

00:54:46.290 --> 00:54:48.859
for whom who saw it as quotas

00:54:48.859 --> 00:54:52.650
and a potential problem
for black Americans.

00:54:52.650 --> 00:54:55.200
Obviously affirmative action
was entirely different.

00:54:55.200 --> 00:54:58.740
So when Jews are citing against it,

00:54:58.740 --> 00:55:00.180
that feels like a betrayal.

00:55:00.180 --> 00:55:05.180
So this complex, you
know, cultural relations,

00:55:05.970 --> 00:55:09.420
but also the, you know,
the relations of habits,

00:55:09.420 --> 00:55:12.663
of thinking and yes,
racial thinking as well.

00:55:14.340 --> 00:55:16.440
- Yeah, so to follow up,

00:55:16.440 --> 00:55:17.970
I'm gonna use moderator's prerogative

00:55:17.970 --> 00:55:19.449
because this is also,

00:55:19.449 --> 00:55:20.790
I've been thinking about
these issues myself.

00:55:20.790 --> 00:55:24.160
To follow up on that question about how

00:55:25.020 --> 00:55:26.400
solidarity could be built between black

00:55:26.400 --> 00:55:29.174
and Jewish communities
in the 21st century.

00:55:29.174 --> 00:55:31.080
One of the things that strikes me, I mean,

00:55:31.080 --> 00:55:35.380
I've read Dubois's essay
"Warsaw Ghetto" many times

00:55:36.576 --> 00:55:40.650
several friends of mine are working on,

00:55:40.650 --> 00:55:44.400
you know, interracial and interfaith,

00:55:44.400 --> 00:55:47.700
anti-fascist organizing in
the thirties and forties.

00:55:47.700 --> 00:55:50.100
And so it does seem like there is this,

00:55:50.100 --> 00:55:53.070
and maybe this helps to explain why

00:55:53.070 --> 00:55:56.370
Baldwin singled out Jewish people

00:55:56.370 --> 00:55:58.590
as the question of whiteness.

00:55:58.590 --> 00:56:00.990
There is this sense that
there was this halcyon day

00:56:00.990 --> 00:56:05.460
of, you know, genuine anti-racist

00:56:05.460 --> 00:56:07.890
and anti anti-Semitic organizing

00:56:07.890 --> 00:56:11.703
and solidarity in the
thirties and forties.

00:56:13.110 --> 00:56:16.260
Was that just a ephemeral moment?

00:56:16.260 --> 00:56:20.133
Is it possible to build
that kind of politics again?

00:56:21.469 --> 00:56:22.890
- So there was-
- Or has too much happened

00:56:22.890 --> 00:56:23.723
since then?

00:56:26.040 --> 00:56:28.320
- Well, a couple of things.

00:56:28.320 --> 00:56:32.370
So I think the, so first
I realize I didn't answer

00:56:32.370 --> 00:56:34.350
the first question about
how to build a solidarity,

00:56:34.350 --> 00:56:38.880
but I think this, your
question as well ties to it.

00:56:38.880 --> 00:56:43.880
I think that we need to,

00:56:44.880 --> 00:56:46.770
we need to understand sort of

00:56:46.770 --> 00:56:48.810
what the enemies are right, in that way.

00:56:48.810 --> 00:56:53.810
And I think understanding the
sort of common roots of that,

00:56:54.540 --> 00:56:58.380
that it's about power and
domination and how it builds

00:56:58.380 --> 00:57:02.520
and how, I mean, in the
story I tell these stories

00:57:02.520 --> 00:57:05.820
don't necessarily intersect
at a certain point,

00:57:05.820 --> 00:57:08.500
but they run on kind of parallel tracks

00:57:09.450 --> 00:57:12.450
because the dynamics of power
and dominations are similar.

00:57:12.450 --> 00:57:15.540
It's just that they are
along religious lines

00:57:15.540 --> 00:57:18.360
and racialized religious lines
on one side of the Atlantic

00:57:18.360 --> 00:57:21.990
and then along racial lines

00:57:21.990 --> 00:57:24.330
on the other side of the Atlantic.

00:57:24.330 --> 00:57:29.330
And when Jews obviously arrived

00:57:29.697 --> 00:57:32.880
in the United States, in 19th century,

00:57:32.880 --> 00:57:36.180
or even earlier in the 18th
century, later 19th century

00:57:36.180 --> 00:57:41.180
after the re revolution
or the 19 early republic,

00:57:42.240 --> 00:57:47.240
they love the idea that
they're seen as equal

00:57:49.440 --> 00:57:52.410
because religion is part
of the protected clause

00:57:52.410 --> 00:57:53.860
and the constitution, right?

00:57:53.860 --> 00:57:57.480
Whereas the system of law is very racial

00:57:57.480 --> 00:58:02.480
in the United States, it's
set along racial lines.

00:58:02.490 --> 00:58:07.490
And I think that one of the
things that might help people

00:58:10.230 --> 00:58:12.450
sort of understand that A, the dynamics

00:58:12.450 --> 00:58:16.740
of power and oppression are built out

00:58:16.740 --> 00:58:20.130
of the same vocabulary
and the same concepts,

00:58:20.130 --> 00:58:25.130
but two, when we have
that the racial discourse

00:58:25.740 --> 00:58:30.740
of Jesus white is flattening
some of that complexity.

00:58:31.440 --> 00:58:34.380
And at the same time
the denial, oh no, no,

00:58:34.380 --> 00:58:35.490
Jews are not white.

00:58:35.490 --> 00:58:40.140
This kind of reflexive denial
also flattens some of the,

00:58:40.140 --> 00:58:42.600
and some of that complexity

00:58:42.600 --> 00:58:47.600
because there is this
legal aspect of again,

00:58:48.630 --> 00:58:52.203
becoming citizens and therefore
having political rights,

00:58:53.190 --> 00:58:54.783
being able to vote.

00:58:55.680 --> 00:58:59.970
And with that comes a lot of,

00:58:59.970 --> 00:59:04.593
there is a, there's meaning
to the word political capital.

00:59:05.970 --> 00:59:10.740
And when a whole population
like black Americans

00:59:10.740 --> 00:59:14.577
were targeted at this franchisement

00:59:14.577 --> 00:59:18.780
and we see today as well with
the various gerrymandering

00:59:18.780 --> 00:59:21.780
to exclude black populations from voting

00:59:21.780 --> 00:59:23.823
or limit the power of their vote,

00:59:25.290 --> 00:59:27.600
that political capital is smaller.

00:59:27.600 --> 00:59:30.990
And I, in the book, I talk
about the sort of the reaction

00:59:30.990 --> 00:59:33.450
and what happened to
the exclusion of Jews,

00:59:33.450 --> 00:59:36.780
of one Jewish individual from hotel

00:59:42.300 --> 00:59:46.350
and in both in Saratoga Springs

00:59:46.350 --> 00:59:48.870
and then later on the Lake Plaza incident

00:59:48.870 --> 00:59:53.073
with Melville Dewey of
the Jewish decimal system.

00:59:54.060 --> 00:59:57.720
There is this political
capital that Jewish citizens

00:59:57.720 --> 01:00:01.980
can use and organize and push for change

01:00:01.980 --> 01:00:04.200
that black individuals didn't have

01:00:04.200 --> 01:00:06.900
because they didn't,
they were, their voting

01:00:06.900 --> 01:00:08.790
and their political power is restricted.

01:00:08.790 --> 01:00:12.000
So I think opening up

01:00:12.000 --> 01:00:14.220
and having these
conversations is important

01:00:14.220 --> 01:00:19.220
on laying this out and
showing some of the habits

01:00:21.750 --> 01:00:25.590
of thinking about both groups,
that those dominant groups

01:00:25.590 --> 01:00:30.570
that both were in which both,

01:00:30.570 --> 01:00:34.073
both groups were well
oppressed in some ways

01:00:34.073 --> 01:00:36.303
is also important to understand.

01:00:38.070 --> 01:00:39.523
- Well we are-
- Oh, the halcyon

01:00:39.523 --> 01:00:43.260
of the 1940s, whether
that was a moment, yes.

01:00:43.260 --> 01:00:46.500
So one of the thing is
that again, this comes in

01:00:46.500 --> 01:00:51.500
because the Nazis used
American racial laws as a model

01:00:54.750 --> 01:00:58.620
for the Nuremberg laws against Jews.

01:00:58.620 --> 01:01:01.980
And this is in the 1930s when we had,

01:01:01.980 --> 01:01:05.580
when Nazis are denaturalizing Jews,

01:01:05.580 --> 01:01:10.580
when they're limiting
the right of citizenship,

01:01:11.040 --> 01:01:13.440
when they're introducing
miscegenation laws

01:01:13.440 --> 01:01:15.330
and so on so forth,

01:01:15.330 --> 01:01:18.750
where black Americans
are seizing the moment

01:01:18.750 --> 01:01:22.830
and saying, look, this is what
we've been going here, right?

01:01:22.830 --> 01:01:27.830
It's the moment of common cause and moment

01:01:28.980 --> 01:01:33.750
that that helps highlight
the plight of black Americans

01:01:33.750 --> 01:01:34.583
in the moment.

01:01:34.583 --> 01:01:37.300
And certainly Jewish intellectuals

01:01:38.550 --> 01:01:43.230
are part of that and they're seeing this.

01:01:43.230 --> 01:01:47.790
And then, you know, just
as there is this discomfort

01:01:47.790 --> 01:01:51.033
with Jewish power for different reason,

01:01:52.110 --> 01:01:55.080
there was this discomfort with black power

01:01:55.080 --> 01:01:58.680
when black power emerged and
we see these sort of fissures

01:01:58.680 --> 01:02:03.610
and breakdown and again, and some, again,

01:02:05.040 --> 01:02:08.520
not all Jews were on, you know,

01:02:08.520 --> 01:02:10.710
on board with civil rights and all that.

01:02:10.710 --> 01:02:13.830
Some Jews were and some
then left the bandwagon,

01:02:13.830 --> 01:02:14.823
some stayed.

01:02:15.750 --> 01:02:20.100
And I think thinking along the lines

01:02:20.100 --> 01:02:22.410
of both the different views

01:02:22.410 --> 01:02:26.310
and not seeing Jews as
a one group with a white

01:02:26.310 --> 01:02:29.198
or always, you know, on the side of

01:02:29.198 --> 01:02:31.233
one struggle or the other.

01:02:32.370 --> 01:02:35.310
And the other thing,

01:02:35.310 --> 01:02:37.650
or I had another thought
that wanted to say,

01:02:37.650 --> 01:02:40.680
but it escaped me make and I come back.

01:02:40.680 --> 01:02:42.840
- Well, I mean we are actually at time.

01:02:42.840 --> 01:02:44.820
- Okay, so it won't come back.

01:02:44.820 --> 01:02:47.910
- No, but, but you know, I'm on a on,

01:02:47.910 --> 01:02:51.060
I'll offer one concluding
thought of my own

01:02:51.060 --> 01:02:54.960
that one of the great lessons
of "Christian Supremacy",

01:02:54.960 --> 01:02:56.520
which again is a fantastic book,

01:02:56.520 --> 01:02:58.200
I highly recommend everybody
in the audience go out

01:02:58.200 --> 01:03:02.340
and buy a copy, is that politics,

01:03:02.340 --> 01:03:05.266
especially group politics are complex,

01:03:05.266 --> 01:03:08.910
but they are sort of
structured and mediated by,

01:03:08.910 --> 01:03:10.203
oh no, did this.

01:03:12.000 --> 01:03:12.833
Did you want?

01:03:12.833 --> 01:03:13.977
- No, no, no, finish your thought, finish.

01:03:13.977 --> 01:03:16.320
- Oh, no, but they're
structured, mediated by

01:03:16.320 --> 01:03:18.690
received law, received ideology.

01:03:18.690 --> 01:03:20.730
We sort of received structures.

01:03:20.730 --> 01:03:21.900
Did you, did you remember or?

01:03:21.900 --> 01:03:24.690
- Yes, my last thought was that, you know,

01:03:24.690 --> 01:03:26.528
in terms of common moment

01:03:26.528 --> 01:03:31.528
and again, how our language might careful

01:03:31.830 --> 01:03:35.610
is this question, you know,
sometimes black antisemitism

01:03:35.610 --> 01:03:37.440
or Jewish racism.

01:03:37.440 --> 01:03:41.340
And I think we have to
kind of move away from that

01:03:41.340 --> 01:03:46.340
and ask ourselves whether there
is anything particular about

01:03:47.040 --> 01:03:49.590
anti-Jewish sentiments
within the black community

01:03:49.590 --> 01:03:52.470
that we could call it black antisemitism

01:03:52.470 --> 01:03:55.920
or whether it is just
antisemitism, some that,

01:03:55.920 --> 01:04:00.360
or anti-Jewish animus that
exists within some group

01:04:00.360 --> 01:04:02.670
some members of the community.

01:04:02.670 --> 01:04:06.270
And similarly, Jewish
racism, is that such a thing

01:04:06.270 --> 01:04:11.270
as Jewish racism or is
its some individuals

01:04:11.550 --> 01:04:15.450
who may express racism
or believe racist ideas?

01:04:15.450 --> 01:04:20.163
And are racism, is it
Jewish or is it just racism?

01:04:21.030 --> 01:04:25.620
So I think one of the
ways to maybe bridge is

01:04:25.620 --> 01:04:30.620
to give up these labels
of pathologizing prejudice

01:04:31.140 --> 01:04:35.820
by adding the adjectives before them

01:04:35.820 --> 01:04:38.760
and actually analyze and unpack them,

01:04:38.760 --> 01:04:43.760
whether it is just simply
a form of prejudice,

01:04:44.130 --> 01:04:46.441
bigotry on one group or the other

01:04:46.441 --> 01:04:47.508
and not pathologize it

01:04:47.508 --> 01:04:50.673
as if it was a single
singular of the other.

01:04:51.600 --> 01:04:53.790
- Well I think that's a
fantastic note to end on.

01:04:53.790 --> 01:04:56.370
I wanna thank you again,
Professor Teter for the talk.

01:04:56.370 --> 01:04:58.050
I wanna thank everybody in the audience

01:04:58.050 --> 01:04:59.220
for coming out this afternoon.

01:04:59.220 --> 01:05:00.480
I especially wanna thank

01:05:00.480 --> 01:05:02.580
because I've been looking
over the guest list,

01:05:02.580 --> 01:05:04.620
all of my students who
have logged on today.

01:05:04.620 --> 01:05:08.580
I said this morning in class,
you should come to this talk

01:05:08.580 --> 01:05:10.020
and I'm glad at least some of you did.

01:05:10.020 --> 01:05:13.230
And so thank you all so, so much.

01:05:13.230 --> 01:05:16.050
And we will see everyone
hopefully sometime soon

01:05:16.050 --> 01:05:17.760
at the next event.

01:05:17.760 --> 01:05:19.530
- Thank you so much for having me.

01:05:19.530 --> 01:05:21.780
Thank you, the students
and everybody for coming.

01:05:21.780 --> 01:05:22.861
Thank you.

01:05:22.861 --> 01:05:23.694
